2186544047 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544067 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544052 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544067 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544067 1077 "selection" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544067 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544019 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544067 1079 #uuid "e6aa17b9-5d07-5942-b17f-d9ecdeadf17f" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544064 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544068 1069 "q-144238" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544068 1071 "Simplify the expression $3w-(w-2).$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544068 1072 #uuid "0943c573-bd9d-551a-bfee-908fd709f4d5" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544068 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n3w-w+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w-w+2 & = \\\\ (3w-w)+2 & = \\\\ 2w+2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544067 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544069 1069 "q-43203" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544069 1071 "${0}^{100}=$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544069 1072 #uuid "13009f1d-d35d-5b85-be9b-7aed8c27ac84" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544069 1074 "The expression ${0}^{100}$ means $0$ multiplied by itself $100$ times. So, since $0$ times any number is $0$,  we have ${0}^{100}=0.$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544065 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544070 1069 "q-99141" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544070 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{{(-625)}^{2}}?$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544070 1072 #uuid "2b01d068-301b-53f3-a201-6640d9320d3d" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544070 1074 "Remember that by definition, the square root is always zero or positive, never negative.\n\nSo, the square root comes out to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-625)^{2}}=\\sqrt{{625}^{2}}=625.\n$$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544066 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544071 1069 "q-160571" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544071 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(-3,-2)$  and $(-3,-7)?$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544071 1072 #uuid "5eb05574-37e3-55ae-bfb9-7d07106c577c" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544071 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/fa/fa516432771fd242ed51ba8548dd04ca88991fc14992be811a53f8e124b9ff7d.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $5$ units, so the distance equals $5.$\n\n![](images/99/999cdc70474e5b928b28d611d4c66fb3eeea5716f516af41574712337cb6f9e9.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/91/91bd9f4cd88e966b796f97184eee8aa791e2f20301ff700906ded4d4731eaaf7.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,-7)$ is at a distance of $7$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,-2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $7-2=5.$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544060 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544072 1069 "q-93124" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544072 1071 "A tank with a capacity of $2\\,000$ liters is filled with $1\\,400$ liters of water. What percentage of the tank is filled with water?" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544072 1072 #uuid "65844bd9-30e0-51e9-84e5-199c9f665392" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544072 1074 "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the amount of water, which is $1\\,400$ liters, and\n\n- the whole is the capacity of the tank, which is $2\\,000$ liters.\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=1\\,400÷2\\,000=\\frac{14}{20}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{20}=\\frac{14\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{70}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{70}{100}=70%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70%$ of the tank is filled with water." 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544062 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544073 1069 "q-53034" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544073 1071 "![](images/cd/cd63e1c0cc60e53240ad8e064add8bd7c4d2abb1971de69a10b8f414b9c92fc8.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a square?" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544073 1072 #uuid "8e575533-586b-5027-be32-c520f60008c9" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544073 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a square is a quadrilateral where all angles are right and all sides are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rhombus. Its four sides are congruent, however, it has no right angle. Therefore, it is not a square.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a trapezoid. The sides are not all congruent, so it is not a square.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a kite. Since the sides are not all congruent, it is not a square.\n\n- The shape $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid. It has sides that are not congruent, therefore, it is not a square.\n\nTherefore, none of the shapes is a square." 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544063 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544074 1069 "q-113970" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544074 1071 "![](images/76/76d3a2d884ab6df8d6b6b97be5d5bac91ab30976ba0a6ef8aacc5f3a30eead6c.png)\n\nConsider the figure shown above. Which of the following triplets of points are collinear?\n\nI. $M,T,N$\n\nII. $S,T,O$\n\nIII. $P,Q,N$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544074 1072 #uuid "bc7ca1bb-54ce-5dca-bfc4-11a992a5eb47" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544074 1074 "- $M,T,N$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/a3/a3574ff790eadbc561d842f7c2b485d055a76f5781da76afbd7fb39e45e7ccf0.png)\n\n- $S,T,O$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/4c/4ca9c5bbfc7c0f95a5bc03e6e84102eb362cba63d937a184089584a7c2ad58bf.png)\n\n- $P,Q,N$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/6b/6b5e9a5c1e1fa271c93667c35c117b197cec3d5a25e5ac311e77f0021663163d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544059 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544075 1069 "q-162123" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544075 1071 "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{\\bbox[1pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{2}\n$$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544075 1072 #uuid "d99374cc-b8d8-56af-8d1d-9419fba84ed0" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544075 1074 "A negative number can be obtained if we divide two numbers, one of which is positive and the other negative.\n\nSo, given a negative fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{-9}{2}=\\frac{9}{-2}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the denominator on the right-hand side is positive $(2).$ Therefore, the missing numerator must be $(-9):$\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{-9}{2}\n$$" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544076 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544079 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544076 1062 #uuid "1353d738-7b7f-55db-a226-b3f2ae72c15a" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544076 1063 #edb/ref 17592186533991 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544077 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544087 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544077 1062 #uuid "153eb166-3531-50fe-8719-936a417e97c1" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544077 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534064 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544078 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544089 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544078 1062 #uuid "21de48e5-badc-5642-bc90-55cfd546dad3" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544078 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544071 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544079 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544092 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544079 1062 #uuid "3c88f808-ff66-53e1-a160-06238f17f29e" 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544079 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544074 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544080 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544083 13194139535934 17592186045417 true] [17592186544080 1062 #uuid "4cbcf276-c0b8-51a7-83b0-92eb1e62729